                <pb facs="0636" n="538" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg538"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Choate to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram in cipher—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-01-01"><hi rendition="#italic">January 1,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>[Mr. Choate states that the only information the foreign office has regarding the
                    seizure of the British vessel <hi rendition="#italic">Mashona</hi> is that the
                    vessel was arrested by H. M. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Partridge</hi> with
                    1,700 bags of flour marked <pb facs="0637" n="539" xml:id="pg539"/> “Z. A. R.”
                    and was brought into prize court for trading with the enemy. It is claimed by
                    the owners that the flour was not destined for the South African Republic]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
